&lt;h4&gt;


  So, Which One?
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Move to 1.0. A surface arrives in one message, the model writes Express instead of JSON, the server validates before anything ships, and one surface can mix the standard Basic catalog with your own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 1.0, write a macro for anything the agent shows often. The model's output drops to one line with the codes and an amount, the figures come from the source on the server, and the cost no longer grows with the data. Rate cards, history charts and comparisons all take that path here. Plain Express covers layouts the macros do not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Budget time for the breaking changes in Change 6, and for a renderer that speaks 1.0: &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/react&lt;/code&gt; 0.15.0 here, in place of CopilotKit's 0.9 renderer.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


  Summary
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal of this article was to show what changed between A2UI 0.9 and A2UI 1.0, how generated UI works on 1.0, and why it is better, using one currency agent built on both. The key to the solution was the 1.0 agent SDK's two additions on top of the protocol: the model writes Express that the server compiles and validates, and server-side macros build the components from live data so the figures never pass through the model. The results were:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 A rate card is 223 tokens of 0.9 JSON, 139 tokens of Express and 21 tokens as a macro call, counted by the Gemini tokenizer&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 A 90-day chart is 1,060 tokens of 0.9 JSON and 21 as a macro call; Express alone saves little when the tokens are data&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 The same Express block compiles to one message on 1.0 and three on 0.9&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 One surface mixed two catalogs, each component naming its own &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 Ten prompts, ten runs each: 100 of 100 replies rendered clean on &lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; and 100 of 100 on &lt;code&gt;gemini-2.5-flash&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 The browser code shrank from 309 lines to 150 once the parser and normalizer went&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 A Rust port passed 132 of 135 Express compiler cases and 38 of 38 response parser cases; the 3 it skips use a 0.9 catalog&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;⚠️ 1.0 JSON that mixes catalogs repeats &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt; on every component and was the largest form of the comparison, at 522 tokens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;❌ 1.0 drops &lt;code&gt;theme&lt;/code&gt; and the h1–h5 Text variants, and renames &lt;code&gt;path&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;call&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;@path&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;@call&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scope: one laptop running all four services, &lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;gemini-2.5-flash&lt;/code&gt; at their default settings, ECB reference rates from the Frankfurter API, measured on 2026-11-11 with ten runs per prompt per model. The token counts compare forms of the same components built by the server: the 0.9 JSON is those components written in the 0.9 shape, not text captured from the 0.9 build's model, and properties left at their defaults are removed from every form. Protocol details come from the 0.9 and 1.0 schemas in &lt;code&gt;a2ui-core&lt;/code&gt; 0.6.0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The strategy for moving a generative UI agent from A2UI 0.9 to 1.0 was validated with an incremental step by step approach.&lt;/p&gt;




